Upgrade procedure for device-tree

Source: the Linux kernel's device-tree files as split out by the
	devicetree-rebasing repository,
	https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/devicetree/devicetree-rebasing.git
	Tag vX.Y-dts matches Linux vX.Y.  The tags are annotated and
	PGP-signed.  v5.10-dts is tag object
	72ed6ce5083e6cef04a7e4f55352b21213db550e on commit
	11cb57a76a466a3277dc71518d980a97cc501609, tree
	289209d0d151c2638f692b172b7e5aa9b76f6a7a.

Current version: Linux 5.10 (vendor/device-tree/5.10).

Fetch only the tag you need into a scratch repository, never into the
src repository:

	git init --bare dt.git
	git -C dt.git fetch --depth 1 \
	    https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/devicetree/devicetree-rebasing.git \
	    tag vX.Y-dts

To verify the tag, check its signature (git verify-tag, with the
signer's key) and compare its tree with an independent copy.  FreeBSD
imports the same tags with the same layout on its vendor/device-tree
branch, reachable from the second parent of its "Update DTS files from
Linux vX.Y" merges (FreeBSD's 5.10 vendor import is e7ffa3b5ce04).  The
tree of our vendor commit must equal the tree of FreeBSD's vendor
commit for the same version.

Layout and pruning
------------------

The vendor branch (vendor/device-tree) holds the contents of the
devicetree-rebasing tag at the top level (Bindings/, include/, src/,
scripts/, COPYING, Makefile, README).  It is merged into
sys/contrib/device-tree.  The 5.9 import was taken from FreeBSD, and
the convention it uses is kept:

  - Each import is laid over the previous vendor tree.  Files that
    upstream deleted are NOT removed.  The stale files build up over
    time; with 5.10 there are 224 of them, mostly old .txt bindings and
    a few .dts/.dtsi files (for example
    src/arm64/rockchip/rk3399-rock-pi-4.dts, which upstream split into
    rk3399-rock-pi-4a/4b/4c.dts in 5.10 but sys/modules/dtb/rockchip
    still builds).
  - The top-level .gitignore is not imported.  Bindings/.gitignore is.
  - scripts/filter.sh is kept at the version carried over from the 5.9
    import.  It is the newer copy from devicetree-rebasing master and
    differs from the tag only by the FILTER_BRANCH_SQUELCH_WARNING
    line.  Nothing in our build uses scripts/.

With this convention the vendor tree equals FreeBSD's vendor tree for
the same version, which is the cross-check above.  If we ever switch to
pristine trees (applying upstream deletions), do it in a vendor commit
of its own, and first check that sys/modules/dtb does not build any of
the deleted files.

To upgrade to a new version:

1. Fetch and verify the tag as described above.
2. Build the new vendor tree: extract the previous vendor commit into
   an empty directory, extract the new tag over it, delete the
   top-level .gitignore and restore scripts/filter.sh from the previous
   vendor commit.  Stage it with a temporary index on top of the
   previous vendor commit:

	GIT_INDEX_FILE=/tmp/idx git read-tree vendor/device-tree
	GIT_INDEX_FILE=/tmp/idx git --work-tree=<dir> add -A -f .
	GIT_INDEX_FILE=/tmp/idx git write-tree

   (-f matters: the tag contains files that our .gitignore would
   otherwise skip).  Check that the only differences from the tag
   (git diff --name-status vX.Y-dts <tree>) are the carried stale
   files (A), .gitignore (D) and scripts/filter.sh (M), with no mode
   changes, and compare the tree with FreeBSD's vendor commit.
   Commit it on vendor/device-tree as "Import device-tree from linux
   X.Y" and tag it vendor/device-tree/X.Y ("linux device tree X.Y").
3. Merge it into sys/contrib/device-tree with a real merge whose second
   parent is the vendor commit (message "Merge commit '<sha>'").  The
   local patches below are the usual conflicts.  FreeBSD's merge of
   the same version is a good reference: after resolving, compare
   sys/contrib/device-tree with FreeBSD's tree at its merge.
4. Update freebsd,dts-version in sys/dts/freebsd-compatible.dts.  The
   arm and arm64 kernels compile it in as LINUX_DTS_VERSION
   (sys/conf/Makefile.arm, Makefile.arm64) and warn at boot if the
   DTB's version differs.
5. Check sys/modules/dtb/*/Makefile against src/ (renamed or split
   boards) and look at FreeBSD's driver changes made for the same
   import.
6. Build arm (armv7), arm64 and riscv GENERIC with their dtb modules
   (MODULES_EXTRA in the GENERIC configs) before and after the update,
   and compare the decompiled DTBs (dtc -I dtb -O dts) for compatible
   strings, clock/reset indices and properties that our drivers use.
   The dt-bindings headers included by drivers in sys/arm, sys/arm64,
   sys/riscv and sys/dev must not renumber existing constants.

Branch policy
-------------

Only the arm, arm64 and riscv kernels use these files.  Keep the
device-tree version in step with the arm/arm64/riscv drivers, which
come from FreeBSD.  FreeBSD stable/13 ships Linux 5.9.  FreeBSD main
merged 5.10 on 2021-03-23, after stable/13 had branched, and kept it
until it merged 5.11 to 5.13 on 2021-07-01.  5.10 is therefore one step
ahead of our FreeBSD 13-based drivers.  FreeBSD made no driver changes
for 5.10 apart from the dts-version bump (1240fba44d74).  Going to 5.11
or later, and especially to 6.x, requires porting the matching FreeBSD
14 driver changes first.  Upgrade master and stable/4.1 together;
stable/4.0 stays on 5.9.

Local patches
-------------

These are applied on top of upstream and are kept by the merge (both
come from FreeBSD and were added here in 7f8df441d2, "add fixes"):

  - src/arm/am33xx-l4.dtsi, include/dt-bindings/pinctrl/omap.h:
    FreeBSD 78abc9e2e6ed reverts upstream commit 27c90e5e48d0, which
    set #pinctrl-cells = <2> on am33xx_pinmux and changed the
    AM33XX_PADCONF() macro to emit two cells.  Per the pinctrl-single
    binding, two cells are only valid with pinctrl-single,bits, not
    with pinctrl-single,pins; with the revert the BeagleBone Black
    boots further.  We keep #pinctrl-cells = <1> and
    AM33XX_PADCONF(pa, dir, mux) emitting one cell ((dir) | (mux)).
    Linux 5.10 also made AM33XX_IOPAD() append a second "(0)" cell;
    that is reverted too (AM33XX_IOPAD(pa, val) emits (val) only).
    FreeBSD resolved its 5.10 merge the same way.
  - src/arm/am33xx-l4.dtsi: FreeBSD 19775aa7bc35 re-applies FreeBSD
    f81b2b9a8abd ("dts: patch the am33xx dts for upcoming clock
    support"), which adds clocks = <&timerN_fck> and
    clock-names = "fck" to the timer3 to timer7 nodes.

Compare sys/contrib/device-tree with the vendor tag to see the local
diff:

	git diff vendor/device-tree/X.Y HEAD:sys/contrib/device-tree
